## Rules:

- Member variables follow hungarian notation and will start with the prefix m_. Known types are:
	- m_iHealth = byte, short, long, long long, int, int8_t, uint8_t, int16_t, uint16_t, int32_t, uint32_t, int64_t, uint64_t.
	- m_fPercentage = float, double.
	- m_bGotHit = bool.
	- m_mDictionary = map, unordered_map.
	- m_sName = string, char*, const char*.
	- m_fnFoo = function<>.
	- m_eType = enum, enum class.
	- m_pClass = raw pointer, unique_ptr, shared_ptr, weak_ptr.
	- m_aNames = [], Array, vector.
	- m_vPosition = Vector2, IVector2, Vector3, IVector3, Vector4, IVector4.
	- m_cCharacter = char.
	- m_qMessages = queue.
- Arguments follow hungarian notation and will start with the prefix a_. Types are the same as member variables.
- Prefer std::string over (const) char*.
- Prefer std::filesystem::path over std::string.
- Prefer std::function over function pointers.
- Prefer lambdas over std::bind.
- Every method and function (even private ones) need multi-line verbose XML documentation like this <summary>\n/// ...\n/// </summary>\n/// <param name=\"a_...\">\n/// <returns>
- Every class should start with 
	//======================================================================================
	// ClassName
	//======================================================================================
	/// <summary>
	/// brief summary.
	/// </summary>
- The first method in a source file of every class should start with
	//======================================================================================
	// ClassName
	//======================================================================================	
- Every method in a source file should be separated by the same separators.
	//======================================================================================
- Prefer putting implementations in source files, rather than headers.
- Make local variables const if they do not change.
- Pass as const reference when possible.
- Prefer std::unique_ptr over raw pointers and manual allocation (IE malloc, free, new, delete).
- Namespaces should not be nested.
- Prefer early returns over nested if statements.
- All if statements should have braces and must be on new lines.
	Do
		"if (test)
		{
		}"
	instead of 
		"if (test) {}"
- Switch statements and cases should always contain braces.
- Include order should be alphabetical.
- Include order is
	1. Parent class/header class of cpp file. (header of cpp should be relative and start with ./)
	2. External libs and std.
	3. Gallus.
	4. Platform-specific Gallus.
	5. Editor.
- Use constexpr instead of #define when possible.
- Always (try to) initialize variables in the fields.
- The Gallus library should be platform-independent. There will be extra libraries for Win32, DX12 and others.
- Use the Log system instead of printf, cout or other print methods.
- Use lowerCamelCase for local variables (for instance: bool hasBeenKilled;).
- Enums should follow UpperCamelCase for the name and FULL CAPITALS for the values. For instance:
	"enum class Color
	{
		RED,
		BLU
	}"
- constexpr and define variables should be FULL CAPITALS.
	- This does not count for functions.
- Namespace names should be lowercase and snake case.
- Folder structure should also be lower case and snake case.
- Don't use "using namespace" (serialization is an exception to the rule).
- Use ifdefs for platform-specific code (just to show it is platform-dependent).
- Start platform-specific classes with their prefixes, such as Win32Window and DX12Texture.
- Do not use magic numbers.
- Use gallus::core::Data for data allocations instead of byte*, char* or uint8_t*.
- Empty functions with brackets should have the brackets on a new line. Like this:
	"void Test()
	{}"
- When overriding a method, explicitly add the override keyword.
- Friend keyword should be used thoughtfully.
- Mutable keyword should be used thoughtfully.
- Use scoped lock instead of manually locking and unlocking mutexes.
- Use the "Get" prefix for getters and "Set" prefix for Setters (unless the name does not make sense, like "GetDead()" vs "IsDead()" for "m_bDead").
- Do not use more than one default parameter.
- When possible to use a reference, use it instead of a pointer.
- Getters should always try to return const references (unless not possible or if it does not make sense like with integers, floats, etc).
- Use nullptr instead of NULL.
- Check pointers and booleans with "if (killed)" instead of "if (killed == true)". 
- Do not use yoda comparisons.
- Do not make any changes to external libraries.
- (for some reason) prefer FILE* over ofstream.
- Prefix abstract classes with the letter I (interface) such as ISystem, IConfig.
- Prefer classes over structs.
- Prefer setters and getters over public fields.
- Declare all field members (m_*) at the bottom of the class in a private block after methods, e.g.: class Foo { public: void Bar(); private: int m_iValue = 0; };.
- All content inside a namespace block (classes, enums, functions, separators) must be indented with one tab (\t) relative to the namespace braces.
- #pragma once as first line in every header, blank line after.
- Inside BEGIN_SERIALIZE_FIELDS each SERIALIZE_FIELD wrapped with one triple-tab //======================================================================================
- Class order strict public -> protected -> private, fields last in private.
- No methods after fields.
- DECLARE_TYPE / DECLARE_ABSTRACT_TYPE / EDITOR_OBJ / DECLARE_METADATA_CLASS immediately after "class Foo {" before any access label.
- Enum layout brace on new line, values double-tab with trailing comma.
- Switch case format
	switch (..)
	{
		case X:
		{
			break;
		}
	}
- Tabs instead of spaces.
- Empty functions brackets collapsed.
- No blank line between last accessor and next access specifier.
- Enums must start with E such as EResourceType.
- htb_libs are holy and should be treated as libraries and should be included with <>. Code specifically for executables should be included with "".